Every CLS fix is the same idea: **the browser must know how big something will be before it has it.**

```html
<!-- Width/height give an aspect ratio, so space is reserved at layout time -->
<img src="photo.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="...">
```

```css
/* Reserve an ad or embed slot even while it is empty */
.ad-slot {
  min-height: 250px;      /* the known slot height */
  contain: layout;        /* keep any internal change from shifting the page */
}

/* Responsive media that keeps its box before loading */
.embed {
  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  width: 100%;
}

/* Fonts: swap without a size jump */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Inter';
  src: url('/fonts/inter-var.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-display: optional;          /* no swap at all if it is late */
  size-adjust: 105%;               /* match the fallback's metrics */
  ascent-override: 90%;
}

/* Overlays must not push content: take them out of flow */
.cookie-banner { position: fixed; bottom: 0; }
```

The font trio — `size-adjust`, `ascent-override`, `descent-override` — is the underused one. It makes the fallback font occupy almost exactly the same space as the web font, so the swap causes no reflow at all. `font-display: optional` goes further by never swapping late, at the cost of some users never seeing your typeface on a first visit.

And the rule that catches most remaining cases: **anything injected above existing content — banners, alerts, promo bars — must be `position: fixed` or reserved for**, never inserted into flow after paint.
